= Kabotiá (Kabocha Squash)

_Scope: This entry treats_ kabotiá _(Brazilian Portuguese, also spelled_ cabotiá_; from Japanese kabocha かぼちゃ) — the Japanese-style winter squash, principally cultivars of_ Cucurbita maxima _Duchesne (Cucurbitaceae), with some hybrids derived from_ Cucurbita moschata _and parental Andean stock. The drug-and-food is the_ fresh fruit _and its_ seeds. _Status: the squash itself is a_ pre-Columbian American domesticate _(C. maxima — Andean South American;_ C. moschata _— Central American), with a remarkable circum-globalization history: New World squash → introduced to Cambodia by 16th-c. Portuguese traders → exported from Cambodia to Japan by the same Portuguese → developed in Japan as the_ kabocha _cultivar (the name "kabocha" being the Japanese rendering of "Cambodia") → reintroduced to Brazil via 20th-c. Japanese-Brazilian immigration (the largest Japanese diaspora outside Japan, ~1.5 million strong, mostly São Paulo state). Thus_ kabotiá _is a_ trans-Pacific globalization story: _the American crop returns to the Americas via Cambodia, Portugal, Japan, and Japanese-Brazilian agricultural reestablishment. Not classical in any Old World tradition; post-classical Chinese register through Ming-era Cucurbita introduction; deeply elaborated modern Japanese, Korean, and Brazilian culinary traditions._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin / botanical:* The "kabocha" / "kabotiá" category includes:
- *_Cucurbita maxima_ Duchesne* — *the principal kabocha genetic background*; Andean South American origin; the *species also includes Hubbard squash, banana squash, buttercup squash, turban squash*. The wild ancestor (*_C. andreana_*) is South American.
- *Some hybrids with _Cucurbita moschata_* — Central American origin; the species also includes butternut squash, calabaza, Tromboncino.

The kabocha cultivar group within C. maxima includes *Kabocha proper* (the standard dark-green-skinned, deep-orange-fleshed Japanese type), *Delica*, *Kuri kabocha*, *Hokkaido kabocha* (often a small orange-skinned cultivar), and the Brazilian *kabotiá*-labelled hybrids (often C. maxima × C. moschata, sometimes called "Tetsukabuto" in Japanese — "iron helmet" — for the hard skin).

The genus name *Cucurbita* is Latin for "gourd" — used by Pliny and other classical Latin sources for the Old World cucurbits (bottle gourd _Lagenaria_, snake gourd _Trichosanthes_, etc.); the *New World Cucurbita species are not classical Roman* — the Linnaean appropriation of the classical name for the New World genus is post-Columbian.#footnote[Etymology and taxonomy: standard botanical sources; Whitaker & Davis, _Cucurbits: Botany, Cultivation, and Utilization_; modern Cucurbita phylogenetics (Smith, 1997 archaeobotanical evidence on Cucurbita domestication; recent molecular phylogenetic literature).]

*Japanese:* *かぼちゃ (kabocha)* — the canonical Japanese name; *the name itself preserves the canonical history of the plant's arrival in Japan*. Portuguese traders introduced kabocha to Japan from *Cambodia* (Portuguese: *Camboja*) in the 16th c., during the Portuguese Nanban-Asia trade period (the Portuguese had a major trading post at Phnom Penh from the 1550s); the *Japanese rendered "Cambodia" as カボチャ kabocha*, and the squash was thereafter known by the country of its trade-origin rather than its true (American) origin. *The Japanese had no idea the plant was American* — Cambodia was simply the immediate Portuguese trade source. This is one of the cleaner examples of *trade-route plant naming* in global agriculture: the plant traveled *Americas → Spain → Portugal → Cambodia → Japan*, with the name preserving only the *penultimate* leg.

Modern Japanese kanji for kabocha: *南瓜 (kabocha)* — "southern melon" — a *folk-Japanese kanji assignment* reflecting the southern (= Cambodia / Southeast Asia) trade source. Older Japanese also used the rendering *唐茄子 (tōnasu)* — "Chinese / Tang eggplant" — reflecting alternative trade-source associations. Modern Japanese cooking literature usually writes かぼちゃ in hiragana or 南瓜 in kanji interchangeably.#footnote[Japanese kabocha etymology: standard Japanese food-history literature (e.g. Tsuji, _Japanese Cooking_; Shurtleff & Aoyagi, _History of Cucurbits in Asia_; Edo-period agricultural treatises). The Cambodia / Camboja → kabocha naming is documented in 16th-c. Nanban-trade Japanese records. The Portuguese trade route via Phnom Penh is part of the broader Portuguese-Asian maritime trade history.]

*Brazilian Portuguese:* *Kabotiá* (also *cabotiá*, *kabotia*) — the Brazilian rendering of Japanese kabocha; the Brazilian usage was introduced principally via the *Japanese-Brazilian agricultural diaspora* (the *Liberdade* district of São Paulo and the broader interior São Paulo agricultural settlements established from 1908 onwards). *Brazilian alternative names:* *abóbora japonesa* — "Japanese pumpkin"; *abóbora moranga* (a related but distinct C. maxima cultivar); *moranga* (a generic word for some C. maxima squash). The standard modern Brazilian commercial term is *kabotiá* or *abóbora kabotiá*.

*Chinese:* *南瓜 (nánguā)*, "southern melon" — for the C. maxima / C. moschata cultivars broadly; *倭瓜 (wōguā)*, "Japanese melon" — for the kabocha cultivar specifically in some northern Chinese usage. The Cucurbita introduction to China was Ming-era (16th c., via Portuguese trade); not in the canonical Bencao Gangmu in its full form (Li Shizhen 1578 was contemporary with or slightly predates the major Cucurbita introduction). Modern Chinese cooking literature treats 南瓜 as a standard winter squash.

*Sanskrit:* *No classical Sanskrit name for Cucurbita maxima* — the plant is post-Columbian to South Asia. Modern Hindi *कद्दू (kaddu)* — applied broadly to pumpkins and winter squashes (post-classical generic). The classical Sanskrit *कूष्माण्ड (kūṣmāṇḍa)* and the closely related *अलाबु (alābu)* refer to Old World cucurbits (Benincasa hispida — winter melon; Lagenaria siceraria — bottle gourd) — *not* the New World Cucurbita.

*Arabic:* *قَرع (qarʿ)* — pumpkin / squash, broadly; *قرع عسلي (qarʿ ʿasalī)* — "honey pumpkin" — modern coinages applied to introduced Cucurbita varieties; *not classical in the Avicennan canon for C. maxima specifically*. The Old World *qarʿ* of Avicenna refers to *Lagenaria siceraria* (bottle gourd) and *Citrullus lanatus* var. (watermelon), not the New World Cucurbita.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

The Cucurbita species (collectively 南瓜) entered Chinese cuisine and dietetics in the late-Ming period, *post-dating the canonical Bencao Gangmu in its formative treatment* (Li Shizhen, 1578) — although Li Shizhen does include a brief entry on 南瓜 in the _Bencao Gangmu_ (with the recognition that it was a recent foreign introduction). The clinical/dietetic reading is *post-classical* in the strict pre-Ming sense but is preserved in late-Qing and modern Chinese-medicine literature.

*Reading by extension and modern Chinese dietetic register:*
- *Property:* warm (溫) to neutral (平).
- *Flavor:* sweet (甘 gān).
- *Channels:* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃).
- *Actions:* *補中益氣 (bǔ zhōng yì qì)* — tonifies the middle, augments qi; *消炎止痛 (xiāo yán zhǐ tòng)* — reduces inflammation, stops pain (modern register); *解毒殺蟲 (jiě dú shā chóng)* — resolves toxin, kills worms (the seeds, especially — see below).
- *Pumpkin seeds (南瓜子 nánguāzǐ)* — *canonical anthelmintic* in Chinese medicine, particularly for *tapeworm and pinworm* (modern register, supported by cucurbitin amino acid pharmacology); used in modern Chinese clinical parasitology.

*Cautions:* damp-heat patterns; food stagnation; the warming-sweet register can compound damp.

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Not classical in Ayurveda._ Reading by extension: madhura-snigdha-guru rasa-guṇa; uṣṇa (mildly warming) vīrya; madhura vipāka; pacifies Vāta and Pitta; mildly aggravates Kapha. The C. moschata-derived *kaddū* register in modern Indian dietetics treats pumpkin family as moderately heavy nutritive food; karma _balya_, _bṛṃhaṇa_. *Post-classical extension*, flagged.

The *seeds* of Cucurbita have an Ayurvedic-by-extension reading as anthelmintic (krimighna), consistent with the Chinese register and modern phytopharmacology.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Not classical in Tibb_ for the C. maxima / C. moschata Cucurbita. The Old World _qarʿ_ register (bottle gourd Lagenaria, watermelon Citrullus) of Avicenna is different. Modern Unani treats the introduced Cucurbita post-classically. No Qurʾānic or Tibb al-Nabawī provenance specific to C. maxima.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *南瓜湯 (nánguā tāng, pumpkin soup)* — modern dietetic preparation; warming-tonifying.
- *南瓜子 (nánguāzǐ, pumpkin seeds) for parasites* — chewed raw seeds (60–120 g for adults) as anthelmintic; canonical Chinese folk-medical practice with modern phytopharmacological grounding.
- *南瓜粥 (pumpkin congee)* — Spleen-Stomach-supporting daily food.

=== Japanese (the canonical home cuisine)

- *Kabocha no nimono (かぼちゃの煮物)* — *the canonical Japanese simmered kabocha preparation*: kabocha cubes simmered in dashi + shoyu + mirin + sake + sugar; one of the foundational *otsumami* / side-dish preparations of the Japanese daily table.
- *Kabocha tempura* — tempura-fried kabocha slices; classical seasonal-winter preparation.
- *Kabocha no kintoki* — sweetened simmered kabocha.
- *Kabocha korokke* — kabocha croquettes; Meiji-era yōshoku (Japanese-Western fusion).
- *Kabocha curry* — modern Japanese curry preparation.
- *Tōji (winter solstice) — kabocha-and-azuki preparation* — the canonical Japanese winter solstice food (*itoko-ni* — kabocha + azuki red beans simmered together); a *climatically and ritually calibrated solstice preparation*, eaten on December 22 in Japan as part of the *tōji* (winter solstice) tradition. The *yu-yuzu* (yuzu bath) and *itoko-ni* together are the canonical *tōji* observance.

=== Korean

- *Hobakjeon (호박전)* — pumpkin pancakes; a canonical Korean side dish.
- *Hobakjuk (호박죽)* — kabocha-and-rice porridge with red beans; classical autumn-winter Korean comfort food.

=== Brazilian (the kabotiá register)

- *Quibebe (or kibebe)* — *the canonical northeastern Brazilian kabocha-based stew*: kabotiá or moranga simmered with onion, garlic, butter, and sometimes meat; a traditional caboclo and northeastern Brazilian dish.
- *Sopa de kabotiá* — kabocha soup; widely consumed in modern Brazilian cuisine.
- *Kabotiá recheada* — stuffed kabocha (with shrimp, with chicken curry — various modern preparations).
- *Doce de moranga / abóbora* — sweet pumpkin preserve, often with coconut; classical Brazilian doce-de-tacho tradition.
- *Camarão na moranga* — *the canonical Rio de Janeiro / southeastern Brazilian dish*: kabocha hollowed out and stuffed with shrimp in a creamy preparation; baked and served in the kabocha itself.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

The Cucurbita squashes are *warm-temperate to subtropical* crops — adapted to *warm summers, frost-free growing season of 4–5 months, moderate rainfall, well-drained soils*. The plants are productive in the *continental seasonal-extreme* and *subtropical-humid* zones globally; modern cultivation is widespread. See `climates.typ` for the foundational framework.

=== Continental seasonal-extreme (Japan, Korea, China — the canonical modern cuisine zone)

The cool-winter / warm-summer continental seasonal cycle; kabocha is *harvested in autumn*, *stored through winter*, and is *the canonical Japanese winter solstice food* (per the tōji tradition).

*Traditional preparation.* The Japanese *kabocha no nimono* and *itoko-ni* preparations are climatically calibrated *winter-warming-nourishing* dishes; the high carotenoid content provides vitamin A in the dark winter when fresh vegetables are scarce. The *tōji winter-solstice consumption* preserves a *climatically-and-ritually-calibrated* seasonal practice.

*Cares.* The warming-sweet-nourishing profile is climatically apt for cool-to-cold winter consumption; summer use is less classically emphasized (though modern Japanese summer kabocha tempura is common).

=== Subtropical / tropical Brazilian (the kabotiá register)

Modern Brazilian cultivation is widespread across all climatic zones; the food register is established across Brazilian regional cuisines. The Brazilian *quibebe* and *camarão na moranga* preparations are climatically widely transposable.

=== Modern transposition

Modern globalized cuisine has spread kabocha / kabotiá across all climates. The dish's gentle dietetic profile makes it broadly suitable; the classical Japanese winter solstice register provides the most-elaborated climatic-cultural tradition.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Approximate composition (cooked kabocha squash, per 100 g):*
- Energy ~30 kcal; Water ~91 g
- Carbohydrate ~7 g; fiber ~1.5 g
- Protein ~1 g; Fat ~0.1 g
- *Minerals:* potassium ~340 mg, magnesium ~14 mg, manganese, copper
- *Vitamins:* *vitamin A very high* (~5500 IU as β-carotene — major source); vitamin C; vitamin E
- *Carotenoids:* β-carotene (deep orange color), lutein, zeaxanthin (eye-health register); *significantly higher carotenoid than butternut* (per some analyses)
- *Phenolic compounds:* modest
- *Glycemic index:* moderate-low; favorable diabetic-dietary register
- *Seeds:* high in zinc, magnesium, healthy fats, *cucurbitin amino acid* (the canonical anthelmintic compound)#footnote[Composition data: USDA FoodData Central; Japanese kabocha data MAFF; carotenoid analyses in _Journal of Agricultural and Food Chemistry_.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Vitamin A precursor* — high β-carotene; significant dietary source.
- *Cucurbitin (in seeds) — anthelmintic* — preclinical and clinical support for tapeworm and pinworm; modern Chinese clinical parasitology uses pumpkin seeds.
- *Phytosterols (in seeds)* — modest cholesterol-modifying effect.
- *Modest glycemic effect* — favorable for diabetic dietary management.
- *Antioxidant* — substantial in the flesh.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Culinary register:* unrestricted at normal dietary intake.
- *Carotenemia* — possible with very heavy daily intake (multiple servings per day for weeks); harmless and reversible.
- *Seeds for parasites:* 60–120 g chewed raw seeds for adults (Chinese clinical practice); pediatric doses proportional; clinician supervision for parasitic infestation.
- *Pregnancy:* safe.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Dose ranges: standard nutrition and Chinese clinical literature; cucurbitin pharmacology in standard parasitology references.]

